Chapter: ProbeShiftCount
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 What does a shift by 64 do?

 Findings 28 and 29 both came from asking of a helper "does it FAIL on the
 same inputs", rather than "does it compute the same answer". This is that
 question asked of the shifts, and it is the first place the answer is
 expected to run the OTHER way -- us stricter than the oracle, killing a
 program bare metal runs.

 Bare metal emits no guard. `emit-bit-shift` (Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:1180)
 moves the count into RCX and emits `shl`/`sar`/`shr` in their CL form, and
 x86 masks CL to six bits for a 64-bit operand. So `bit-shl a 64` is
 `bit-shl a 0` and answers `a` unchanged; `bit-shl a 65` shifts by one; a
 negative count shifts by its low six bits, so -1 shifts by 63. Nothing traps
 and nothing says anything.

 `cx_shl` is `a << @as(u6, @intCast(b))`. `@intCast` of 64 to a u6 does not
 fit, and `@intCast` of a negative to an unsigned does not either, so both
 panic in Debug -- which is the mode every native and every emitted rung is
 built in. In a release mode they are undefined behaviour instead, which is a
 third answer again and worth stating plainly: **our guarantee here is a
 property of the build mode, where bare metal's is a property of the emitted
 instruction.** Finding 27 already showed that distinction biting, when a
 memset elided in ReleaseFast changed what a fresh span contained.

 So the prediction is that bare metal prints all nine lines and this arm dies
 at the first out-of-range shift. The in-range assertions come first so that
 dying costs the least information, and the out-of-range lines are ordered
 one operator at a time.

 The masked answers are printed as integers rather than as yes/no, because
 the number IS the finding: `1 << 64` answering 1 is what masking looks like,
 and a relational assertion would hide which of the two behaviours produced
 a true.

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- in range, expected identical on both arms --"
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by 0    " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 0))
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by 4    " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 4))
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by 62   " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 62))
   print-line-uni ("shr 256 by 4  " & integer-to-text (bit-shr 256 4))
   print-line-uni ("shr -256 by 4 " & integer-to-text (bit-shr (0 - 256) 4))
   print-line-uni ("shru 256 by 4 " & integer-to-text (bit-shru 256 4))
   print-line-uni ("shr is arith  " & show-yn (bit-shr (0 - 8) 1 == (0 - 4)))
   print-line-uni "-- out of range: x86 masks the count to six bits --"
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by 64   " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 64))
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by 65   " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 65))
   print-line-uni ("shr 256 by 68 " & integer-to-text (bit-shr 256 68))
   print-line-uni ("shru 256 by 68" & integer-to-text (bit-shru 256 68))
   print-line-uni "-- negative counts take their low six bits --"
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by -1   " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 (0 - 1)))
   print-line-uni ("shl 1 by -64  " & integer-to-text (bit-shl 1 (0 - 64)))
   print-line-uni "-- reached the end, so this arm masked rather than trapping --"
  end
